# scripts/research/nonmutable/bootc.capabilities — R2 PROTOTYPE, not a fleet declaration
# (NON-MUTABLE-HOST-PROPOSAL.md §5 R2, #1004). What dotfiles-Fedora's os/fedora.capabilities
# WOULD become on Silverblue / a bootc host, written with the existing keys plus the four
# prototype ones, every value either measured on a booted fedora-bootc:42 guest
# (research-nonmutable-vm.yml, runs 34819015395 and 34820719480) or marked "to verify".
#
# The R2 test: does any REQUIRED key end up a lie? Read against os/fedora.capabilities:
#   PKG_INSTALL   dnf resolves 317 packages then "this bootc system is configured to be
#                 read-only" (measured, root) — the mutable verb is a refusal. The honest
#                 verb STAGES (`rpm-ostree install --dry-run` as root: exit 0, measured,
#                 run 34852611338); it is true only with PKG_APPLY beside it.
#   PKG_UPGRADE   `rpm-ostree upgrade` stages a deployment (documented; --check measured as
#                 far as a local-origin disk allows). Same shape.
#   PKG_COUNT_PENDING
#                 the only user-runnable question is "is a deployment staged?" —
#                 `rpm-ostree status --pending-exit-77` answers with its exit status
#                 (measured: exit 0 idle; 77 when staged). The answer is one deployment,
#                 not N packages — PKG_PENDING_EXIT_SOME says so.
# Everything else fills in truthfully. Verdict for this family: additive — no required
# key changes meaning; two optional keys (PROVISIONER, PKG_APPLY) and one exit-status key
# carry the difference.

PROVISIONER=atomic

# ── package-manager verbs (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Layered-package metadata; the base image needs none.
PKG_REFRESH=sudo rpm-ostree refresh-md
# STAGES the next deployment. Non-interactive by nature (no transaction prompt), so
# PKG_ASSUME_YES is omitted: `up -y` behaves like `up`. `up` should print PKG_APPLY.
PKG_UPGRADE=sudo rpm-ostree upgrade
# Layers into the NEXT deployment; nothing is on PATH until PKG_APPLY. --idempotent so a
# re-run over an already-layered package is not an error (the driver's provision hook
# re-runs). `-A` (apply-live) exists and is deliberately not declared: it is documented as
# partial, and a bootstrap that half-applies is the failure mode this schema avoids.
PKG_INSTALL=sudo rpm-ostree install --idempotent
PKG_REMOVE=sudo rpm-ostree uninstall
# Read-only metadata queries work on the deployed host (dnf is present; only the
# transaction is refused). MEASURED on a booted bootc guest (run 35130669056): a hit is
# exit 0 in 1.2 s AS THE USER, and an unprivileged `dnf -q makecache` populates the cache
# first, so the verb needs no escalator here any more than on a mutable host.
# THE CATCH: a MISS is also exit 0 (three lines, "no matches"). Hit and miss are
# indistinguishable by exit status on dnf5, so a consumer must read the output. No consumer
# reads this key's status today — it renders into PORTING-MATRIX.md's verb table — but a
# future "is it available?" reader would get it wrong by assuming the shell convention.
PKG_SEARCH=dnf search
PKG_OWNS=rpm -qf
# R5 (measured): the AVAILABLE question — is a newer image upstream? — has no
# unprivileged answer here: `rpm-ostree upgrade --check` is "AutomaticUpdateTrigger not
# allowed for user" (0.1 s, exit 1) and `bootc upgrade --check` "must be executed as
# root"; as root the first answers in 0.1 s with 77 = unchanged. So no count verb: the
# maint runner's unattended `rpm-ostree upgrade` (safe — inert until the reboot) asks it,
# and the nudge reports the STAGED state below instead. (`dnf -q check-update` does run
# unprivileged, 1.4 s, exit 100 with 30 lines — but it counts RPM updates against the
# deployment's rpmdb, which is not how an image host updates; a false nudge.)
# STAGED: user-runnable, 0.2 s, exit 77 = a deployment is queued for the next boot.
PKG_APPLY_PENDING=rpm-ostree status --pending-exit-77
PKG_APPLY_PENDING_EXIT=77

# ── the staged change becomes live (prototype) ────────────────────────────────
# `rpm-ostree upgrade --reboot` and `bootc upgrade --apply` exist; a plain reboot is the
# one verb that applies BOTH an upgrade and a layered install, so it is the declaration.
PKG_APPLY=sudo systemctl reboot

# ── scheduler (required) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Measured: systemd user units work on the guest unchanged.
SCHEDULER=systemd
SCHEDULER_UNIT_DIR=~/.config/systemd/user

# ── how `up` applies (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# No PKG_ASSUME_YES (nothing prompts). No PKG_UPGRADE_PARTIAL: the image updates as a
# whole — the same safety declaration Arch makes, here by construction.
# A staged upgrade is inert until PKG_APPLY, so an unattended `rpm-ostree upgrade` is
# SAFER than a mutable one: nothing changes under a running session. The runner must
# never run PKG_APPLY; that is the operator's reboot.
MAINT_UNATTENDED_UPGRADE=1

# ── how `up` counts (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing to count: no PKG_COUNT_PENDING (see above), so no PKG_PENDING_MATCH / FIELD / FS.
